  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Sunday)\nIssued at 104 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nWater Vapor satellite trends and this morning's upper air\nanalysis places a mid-level ridge over the eastern U.S. with a\ntrough centered over the Rockies. Latest surface analysis shows a\nsurface low over eastern South Dakota with a trailing cold front\nstretching southwest into southeast Colorado and New Mexico.\nSoutherly return flow on the backside of high pressure centered\noff the Mid-Atlantic coast is transporting low-level moisture up\ninto the Lower Mississippi Valley. Late morning temperatures are\nin the 60s to lower 70s.\n\nThis mild southerly flow will result in mild temperatures across\nthe Mid-South this afternoon in the 70s with lows tonight in the\nupper 50s to lower 60s. The aforementioned upper-level trough\nwill move into the Plains on Tuesday. A tightening pressure\ngradient associated with the surface low and approaching cold\nfront will result in elevated and gusty south winds especially\nalong and west of the Mississippi River. Thus, a Wind Advisory\nhas been issued for portions of northeast Arkansas and the\nMissouri Bootheel from 8 AM to 3 PM Tuesday.\n\nThe upper-level trough and associated cold front will move into\nthe Lower Mississippi Valley Tuesday afternoon into Tuesday\nnight. Short term CAMs suggest a few warm advection rain showers\nmainly west of the Mississippi River during the day but\nconfidence remains too low to include in the forecast at this\ntime. The main concern will be with the convection that develops\nupstream and moves into the Mid-South beginning late Tuesday\nafternoon, then spreading across the remainder of the area into\nTuesday night. Favorable forcing and decent mid-level height\nfalls will spread across the area during this time.  A messy\nmostly linear convective mode is expected across the area with\nlimited surface-based instability up to around 300 J/kg at best\nwith relatively poor mid-level lapse rates. Model soundings with\nthe associated hodographs indicate some low level curvature with\n0-3 km storm relative helicities approaching 300 m2/s2,\nsupporting some embedded circulations within the convective line,\nespecially over north Mississippi Tuesday night. Damaging winds\nwill be the primary threat if any stronger winds can mix down to\nthe surface, with a secondary threat of a tornado. Localized\nheavy rainfall will also be a threat as precipitable water values\nwill be around 1.7 inches. (99th percentile for early November)\n\nDrier air will filter in behind the front by Wednesday with\ntemperatures remaining relatively mild until late this week when\ndeterministic and ensemble model solutions show a couple of mid-\nlevel shortwaves rotating through the eastern US with a secondary\ncold front bringing much cooler temperatures to the region for\nthe upcoming weekend with highs in the 60s and lows in the 30s\nand 40s. NBM probabilities show a medium chance (40-50%) chance\nof minimum temperatures at or below 35 degrees mostly across the\nfavored locations of West Tennessee both Sunday and Monday\nmornings. These cooler temperatures, and light winds may result\nin the development of some frost. Frost freeze headlines have\nended with the end of the climatological growing season.",
